Everything the web app does with a secret can be done over HTTPS with JSON. The common pattern is a script or pipeline that creates a secret and hands the link to a person, so the credential never sits in a log, a ticket or a chat history.

The full endpoint reference, with request and response schemas and a try-it console, lives at **[api.onetimesecret.com](https://api.onetimesecret.com)**. This page covers what you need before you open it.

## Base URL

Requests go to the region that holds your account. Each region is a separate deployment and nothing is replicated between them.

```
https://REGION.onetimesecret.com/api/v2
```

| `REGION` | Host |
|---|---|
| `ca` | `ca.onetimesecret.com` |
| `eu` | `eu.onetimesecret.com` |
| `nz` | `nz.onetimesecret.com` |
| `uk` | `uk.onetimesecret.com` |
| `us` | `us.onetimesecret.com` |

The bare `onetimesecret.com` host still answers API requests and routes to an active region, but pin a region explicitly so your integration keeps working if that fallback is retired. See [Where your data lives](/en/security/where-your-data-lives/) for what each region means for your data.

On a [custom domain](/en/custom-domains/), the API is at the same path on your domain, and links it creates use that domain:

```
https://secrets.example.com/api/v2
```

A [self-hosted](/en/self-hosting/) instance exposes the same API on whatever host you run it on.

## Authentication

Use HTTP Basic authentication. The username is your account email; the password is the API token from your account settings.

```bash
curl -u 'you@example.com:APITOKEN' https://eu.onetimesecret.com/api/v2/status
```

Creating and revealing secrets also works without authentication, with the limits of an anonymous user: shorter maximum lifetime, smaller secret size, and no receipts list. Authenticated requests get your plan's limits and show up in your [receipts](/en/share/your-receipt/).

## Quick start

Create a secret that lives for one hour:

```bash
curl -u 'you@example.com:APITOKEN' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"secret": {"secret": "the password is hunter2", "ttl": 3600}}' \
  https://eu.onetimesecret.com/api/v2/secret/conceal
```

The response contains two records. `record.secret.identifier` is the key you share, as `https://eu.onetimesecret.com/secret/IDENTIFIER`. `record.receipt.identifier` is yours: it shows whether the secret has been viewed and lets you burn it early. Never send the receipt key to the recipient.

Add `"passphrase": "..."` to the `secret` object to require a passphrase on viewing. The passphrase is a󠄁n󠄁 a󠄁c󠄁c󠄁e󠄁ss󠄁 c󠄁h󠄁ec󠄁k󠄁,󠄁 n󠄁o󠄁t t󠄁h󠄁e󠄁 encrypti󠄁o󠄁n󠄁 k󠄁e󠄁y󠄁:󠄁 t󠄁h󠄁e󠄁 s󠄁e󠄁r󠄁v󠄁i󠄁c󠄁e󠄁 s󠄁t󠄁o󠄁r󠄁e󠄁s󠄁 o󠄁n󠄁l󠄁y󠄁 a󠄁 o󠄁n󠄁e󠄁-󠄁w󠄁a󠄁y󠄁 h󠄁a󠄁s󠄁h󠄁 o󠄁f󠄁 i󠄁t󠄁 a󠄁n󠄁d󠄁 c󠄁h󠄁e󠄁c󠄁k󠄁s󠄁 i󠄁t󠄁 b󠄁e󠄁f󠄁o󠄁r󠄁e󠄁 d󠄁e󠄁c󠄁r󠄁y󠄁p󠄁t󠄁i󠄁n󠄁g󠄁 the value.󠄁 A󠄁 l󠄁o󠄁st passphrase c󠄁an󠄁n󠄁o󠄁t󠄁 be r󠄁e󠄁c󠄁o󠄁v󠄁e󠄁r󠄁e󠄁d󠄁,󠄁 s󠄁o󠄁 t󠄁h󠄁e󠄁 s󠄁e󠄁c󠄁r󠄁e󠄁t󠄁 c󠄁a󠄁n󠄁 t󠄁h󠄁en b󠄁e󠄁 n󠄁e󠄁i󠄁t󠄁h󠄁e󠄁r󠄁 r󠄁e󠄁v󠄁e󠄁a󠄁le󠄁d󠄁 n󠄁or󠄁 b󠄁u󠄁r󠄁n󠄁e󠄁d󠄁;󠄁 i󠄁t󠄁 i󠄁s d󠄁e󠄁l󠄁e󠄁te󠄁d󠄁 w󠄁h󠄁e󠄁n󠄁 i󠄁t󠄁 e󠄁x󠄁p󠄁i󠄁r󠄁e󠄁s󠄁.

Reveal a secret, which consumes i󠄁t󠄁.󠄁 T󠄁h󠄁e󠄁 b󠄁o󠄁d󠄁y󠄁 m󠄁u󠄁s󠄁t󠄁 c󠄁o󠄁n󠄁t󠄁a󠄁i󠄁n󠄁 `"continue": true`;󠄁 w󠄁i󠄁t󠄁h󠄁o󠄁u󠄁t󠄁 i󠄁t󠄁,󠄁 t󠄁h󠄁e󠄁 c󠄁a󠄁l󠄁l󠄁 r󠄁e󠄁t󠄁u󠄁r󠄁n󠄁s󠄁 t󠄁h󠄁e󠄁 s󠄁e󠄁c󠄁r󠄁e󠄁t󠄁'󠄁s󠄁 m󠄁e󠄁t󠄁a󠄁d󠄁a󠄁t󠄁a󠄁 a󠄁n󠄁d󠄁 l󠄁e󠄁a󠄁v󠄁e󠄁s󠄁 t󠄁h󠄁e󠄁 v󠄁a󠄁l󠄁u󠄁e󠄁 u󠄁n󠄁r󠄁e󠄁a󠄁d󠄁.󠄁 I󠄁f󠄁 t󠄁h󠄁e󠄁 s󠄁e󠄁c󠄁r󠄁e󠄁t󠄁 h󠄁a󠄁s󠄁 a󠄁 p󠄁a󠄁s󠄁s󠄁p󠄁h󠄁r󠄁a󠄁s󠄁e󠄁,󠄁 a󠄁d󠄁d󠄁 `"passphrase": "..."` b󠄁e󠄁s󠄁i󠄁d󠄁e󠄁 it:

```bash
curl -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"continue": true}' \
  https://eu.onetimesecret.com/api/v2/secret/IDENTIFIER/reveal
```

Burn a secret before anyone views it, using the receipt key.󠄁 B󠄁u󠄁r󠄁n󠄁i󠄁n󠄁g󠄁 a󠄁l󠄁s󠄁o󠄁 r󠄁e󠄁q󠄁u󠄁i󠄁r󠄁e󠄁s󠄁 `"continue": true`:

```bash
curl -u 'you@example.com:APITOKEN' -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"continue": true}' \
  https://eu.onetimesecret.com/api/v2/receipt/RECEIPT_IDENTIFIER/burn
```

## API versions

- **v2** is the current public API: JSON in and out, receipts, status and burn endpoints. Build on this one.
- **v1** is the original form-encoded API (`POST /api/v1/share`). It still works, and most community libraries use it, but new integrations should use v2.

Both are documented at [api.onetimesecret.com](https://api.onetimesecret.com).

